1
00:00:07,425 --> 00:00:10,735
Last time, we sorted living things into four groups.

2
00:00:10,955 --> 00:00:13,055
Today, we zoom in on one group.

3
00:00:13,275 --> 00:00:14,125
Animals!

4
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:15,958
A frog, a bird and a fish.

5
00:00:16,068 --> 00:00:17,328
All at one pond!

6
00:00:17,398 --> 00:00:18,728
Are they one group?

7
00:00:19,151 --> 00:00:20,631
Did you lock in your answer?

8
00:00:20,871 --> 00:00:22,271
One group, or three?

9
00:00:22,521 --> 00:00:23,701
Hold on to it.

10
00:00:24,215 --> 00:00:25,775
It's You against the Buddies.

11
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:27,275
You lock in first.

12
00:00:27,495 --> 00:00:29,235
One finger for every point.

13
00:00:29,435 --> 00:00:31,915
A little animal is hiding in this video.

14
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:33,305
Can you spot it?

15
00:00:34,618 --> 00:00:35,568
Round Zero.

16
00:00:35,788 --> 00:00:36,658
A warm-up!

17
00:00:36,928 --> 00:00:38,658
A plant makes its own food.

18
00:00:38,878 --> 00:00:39,918
Can an animal?

19
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:41,228
One, yes.

20
00:00:41,448 --> 00:00:42,248
Two, no.

21
00:00:42,478 --> 00:00:43,888
Show me with your fingers.

22
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:50,310
Locked in!

23
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:51,325
Two!

24
00:00:51,602 --> 00:00:54,132
Animals are unable to make their own food.

25
00:00:54,342 --> 00:00:56,702
They depend on other living things for food.

26
00:00:56,833 --> 00:00:57,823
Two fingers?

27
00:00:58,053 --> 00:00:59,213
One finger up!

28
00:01:00,072 --> 00:01:01,422
Today you'll learn…

29
00:01:01,551 --> 00:01:03,141
What an animal is.

30
00:01:03,194 --> 00:01:05,254
How scientists group animals.

31
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:07,261
What makes a frog an amphibian.

32
00:01:08,239 --> 00:01:09,159
Are you ready?

33
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:10,339
Three!

34
00:01:10,729 --> 00:01:11,369
Two!

35
00:01:11,739 --> 00:01:12,369
One!

36
00:01:12,769 --> 00:01:13,439
Go!

37
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:15,477
So, what is an animal?

38
00:01:15,757 --> 00:01:16,817
Look at the frog.

39
00:01:17,137 --> 00:01:17,887
A head.

40
00:01:18,177 --> 00:01:19,017
A body.

41
00:01:19,317 --> 00:01:20,227
And limbs.

42
00:01:20,507 --> 00:01:22,007
Its legs are its limbs.

43
00:01:22,669 --> 00:01:23,869
Say it with me!

44
00:01:24,581 --> 00:01:28,481
Animals are living things that usually have parts such as a head,

45
00:01:28,231 --> 00:01:29,671
a body and limbs.

46
00:01:32,294 --> 00:01:36,254
Some animals live only on land, while some live only in water.

47
00:01:36,454 --> 00:01:40,184
There are also some animals that can live both on land and

48
00:01:39,934 --> 00:01:40,704
in water.

49
00:01:41,206 --> 00:01:45,456
Animals have different outer coverings as they live in different places.

50
00:01:48,988 --> 00:01:53,598
Scientists have grouped the diversity of animals around us according to their

51
00:01:53,348 --> 00:01:55,218
similarities and differences.

52
00:01:55,353 --> 00:01:57,333
They use things we can see or measure.

53
00:01:57,553 --> 00:02:00,133
These are called observable characteristics.

54
00:02:04,354 --> 00:02:05,224
Round one.

55
00:02:05,484 --> 00:02:07,134
This frog sits on a leaf.

56
00:02:07,364 --> 00:02:08,484
Then, splash!

57
00:02:08,714 --> 00:02:09,654
It swims.

58
00:02:09,874 --> 00:02:11,224
Where can a frog live?

59
00:02:11,504 --> 00:02:12,974
One, only on land.

60
00:02:13,214 --> 00:02:14,654
Two, only in water.

61
00:02:14,904 --> 00:02:15,904
Three, both.

62
00:02:16,124 --> 00:02:17,534
Show me with your fingers.

63
00:02:23,009 --> 00:02:23,959
Locked in!

64
00:02:24,089 --> 00:02:24,889
Two!

65
00:02:24,919 --> 00:02:26,179
It swims so well.

66
00:02:26,339 --> 00:02:27,909
It must live in water.

67
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:28,809
Three.

68
00:02:29,019 --> 00:02:31,259
I saw it on a leaf, and in the pond.

69
00:02:31,309 --> 00:02:32,029
Three!

70
00:02:32,129 --> 00:02:33,649
It hops, and it swims.

71
00:02:34,145 --> 00:02:35,135
Be the frog!

72
00:02:35,395 --> 00:02:36,365
Shrink down.

73
00:02:36,595 --> 00:02:37,465
Quietly.

74
00:02:39,853 --> 00:02:41,003
You are a frog.

75
00:02:41,353 --> 00:02:43,153
You sit on a leaf, on land.

76
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:45,170
Your skin is moist.

77
00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:47,210
It stops you from drying out.

78
00:02:47,808 --> 00:02:48,848
Now, jump!

79
00:02:50,455 --> 00:02:51,605
You are under water.

80
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:54,825
Your strong legs and webbed feet push you along.

81
00:02:55,344 --> 00:02:57,214
Here, you breathe through your skin.

82
00:02:58,424 --> 00:03:01,284
Back on land, you breathe with your lungs as well.

83
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:03,077
Land and water.

84
00:03:03,457 --> 00:03:04,607
Both are home.

85
00:03:07,013 --> 00:03:08,323
The answer is three.

86
00:03:08,563 --> 00:03:09,323
Both!

87
00:03:09,982 --> 00:03:11,192
Say it with me!

88
00:03:11,907 --> 00:03:16,007
Amphibians are animals that can live both on land and in water.

89
00:03:16,127 --> 00:03:18,407
Amphibians usually have four legs.

90
00:03:22,928 --> 00:03:26,588
Amphibians can breathe through their skin as well as their lungs.

91
00:03:26,722 --> 00:03:30,612
They have protective moist skin to prevent them from drying out when

92
00:03:30,362 --> 00:03:31,452
they are on land.

93
00:03:33,784 --> 00:03:35,964
Amphibians lay their eggs in water.

94
00:03:36,165 --> 00:03:39,115
Frogs, toads and salamanders are amphibians.

95
00:03:39,249 --> 00:03:41,139
So the frog is an amphibian!

96
00:03:42,352 --> 00:03:43,412
Three fingers?

97
00:03:43,632 --> 00:03:44,792
One finger up!

98
00:03:46,543 --> 00:03:47,243
My rule!

99
00:03:46,993 --> 00:03:48,923
If it swims, it is a fish.

100
00:03:48,993 --> 00:03:50,153
A frog swims.

101
00:03:50,213 --> 00:03:51,313
A duck swims.

102
00:03:51,333 --> 00:03:52,493
So they are fish!

103
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:54,310
Something is not right.

104
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:56,620
If you can spot it, cross your arms.

105
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:57,750
Hold on!

106
00:04:03,257 --> 00:04:04,367
Arms crossed?

107
00:04:04,587 --> 00:04:05,727
One finger up!

108
00:04:05,957 --> 00:04:07,367
Many animals swim.

109
00:04:07,587 --> 00:04:10,197
To find the group, look at the outer covering.

110
00:04:10,630 --> 00:04:11,320
Oops.

111
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,070
I must check the cover first!

112
00:04:13,647 --> 00:04:15,037
Show me your score hand!

113
00:04:15,267 --> 00:04:16,277
More than two?

114
00:04:16,507 --> 00:04:17,787
You beat the Buddies.

115
00:04:18,007 --> 00:04:19,067
Three fingers?

116
00:04:19,287 --> 00:04:20,287
High five!

117
00:04:21,084 --> 00:04:22,764
Did you spot the hidden animal?

118
00:04:23,014 --> 00:04:25,444
It was the duck, behind the boardwalk rail!

119
00:04:25,664 --> 00:04:26,934
Tell someone at home.

120
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:29,897
Let's read today's key words together!

121
00:04:30,137 --> 00:04:30,857
Ready?

122
00:04:31,147 --> 00:04:32,257
Outer covering.

123
00:04:33,812 --> 00:04:35,672
Observable characteristics.

124
00:04:38,024 --> 00:04:38,853
Amphibians.

125
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,550
Moist skin.

126
00:04:43,003 --> 00:04:43,903
Well done!

127
00:04:44,951 --> 00:04:46,931
Next: this animal has fins.

128
00:04:47,141 --> 00:04:48,771
But it hops about on the mud!

129
00:04:48,991 --> 00:04:50,811
Is it a fish, or an amphibian?

130
00:04:51,031 --> 00:04:52,511
Find out in Part Two!

131
00:04:54,276 --> 00:04:55,335
Say it with us!

132
00:04:56,135 --> 00:04:56,845
Ready?

133
00:04:56,969 --> 00:04:57,589
One!

134
00:04:57,969 --> 00:04:58,649
two!

135
00:04:58,974 --> 00:05:00,149
Now you know!
